b"> In DeepChem 2.3, MPNNModel (like most other model classes) is a subclass of KerasModel, which is a wrapper around a tf.keras.Model. So you can just use `model.model` to look up that Keras model and call whatever functions you want on it. But I don't see any such method as `count_params()`? https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/Model

Thanks,  i am going to try DeepChem 2.3, Keras model has a method `count_params()`  to get trainable parameters here:https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/backend/count_params

I wrote a function by myself for DeepChem 2.1 here to get MPNN model parameters, not very sure is this correct:

```
def count_params(model):
    trainable_paras = 0
    for layer in model.layers.values():
        x = model.get_layer_variable_values(layer)
        for  i in x:
            if type(i) == np.ndarray:
                if len(i.shape) == 1:
                    trainable_paras += i.shape[0]
                else:
                    a, b = i.shape
                    trainable_paras += a*b
    return trainable_paras
```"

b"@rbharath there's a couple of failing examples that I need your advice on, because I don't know what the intended behavior is.  qm7_tf_model.py and qm7b_tf_model.py each create a MultitaskFitTransformRegressor with `n_evals=10`.  It overrides `predict_on_generator()` to run the prediction `n_evals` times for each batch.  That leads to an exception in `model.evaluate()`, because the array of predicted outputs is 10 times as large as the array of labels.  What's supposed to happen here?

This error didn't come up before because there used to be a bug in MultitaskFitTransformRegressor that caused `n_evals` to effectively be ignored.  Here's how it implemented `predict_on_generator()` in 2.2:

```python
  def predict_on_generator(self, generator, transformers=[], outputs=None):

    def transform_generator():
      for feed_dict in generator:
        X = feed_dict[self.features[0]]
        for i in range(self.n_evals):
          X_t = X
        for transformer in self.fit_transformers:
          X_t = transformer.X_transform(X_t)
        feed_dict[self.features[0]] = X_t
        yield feed_dict

    return super(MultitaskFitTransformRegressor, self).predict_on_generator(
        transform_generator(), transformers, outputs)
```

You can see the obviously incorrect indentation.  The entire loop over `n_evals` is just

```python
        for i in range(self.n_evals):
          X_t = X
```

which is no different from not looping at all.  Everything else below it should also have been in the loop so it would actually evaluate the model multiple times.  I fixed that when I converted it to Keras, and that revealed the problem described above.

What is supposed to be happening?"

