b"This is a good point. I think I understand what you're getting at now.

Let me summarize what I understand of the proposal. I believe the key change required would be that the constructors for layers would take on the responsibility for getting the parents for that given layer. `add_layer` does this right now and stores all parent information in the `TensorGraph`, but the modification would store parent information within the layers themselves. Then, the `TensorGraph` would be responsible for tracing backwards and constructing the DAG and evaluating the DAG to add tensorflow ops to its graph. All tensorflow ops building would remain within the `__call__` methods for layers, which should stay unchanged.

@peastman Is this a correct summary?

In general, I like this proposed change. This would make our code look very similar to Keras's functional API, which is probably the cleanest deep network code style out there. As a bonus, we don't lose the tight control over the Tensorflow graphs that we need.

@lilleswing Does the proposed design change look reasonable to you? Are there any failure cases we might run into?"

b"Keras layers are very complicated under the hood. Part of this is due to Keras's support for both Theano and Tensorflow back-ends. This dual support necessitates multiple abstractions that make back-end Keras code pretty difficult to work with. The current minimal `Layer` objects in `TensorGraph` are much nicer to build on.

I like the idea of making Tensorflow objects that are explicitly exposed to users. There are a lot of new research layers in DeepChem and ideally users should be able to mix DeepChem `Layer` objects with custom Tensorflow code as desired.

@lilleswing Does the current `TensorGraph` API support shared layers?"

